--TEST--
`serverVersion()` is a field read and not a statement, and the proof is where a statement is refused: it answers between two rows of a `stream`, on the connection that walk is holding — `rule:core-classes/db-streaming` and `rule:core-classes/db-connection-busy-state`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::serverVersion
// The busy rule is what makes this observable at all. A stream holds the connection
// for as long as rows remain, so anything that needs a round trip is refused
// between two steps of the walk — and this member is not refused, which is the
// one thing a program can see about a cost it does not pay.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
$db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key, name text)", []);
$db->executeMany("insert into t (name) values (?)", [["ada"], ["grace"]]);

var $kept = $db->serverVersion();

foreach ($db->stream("select name from t order by id", []) as Core\Db\Row $row) {
    echo "row ", $row->string("name") ?? "null", "\n";

    // The version the handshake delivered, read off the held connection: the
    // same string as before the walk opened, because nothing about the walk
    // touches it.
    echo "  version: ", $db->serverVersion() == $kept ? "the same" : "something else", "\n";

    // The comparison the case exists for. A statement here is the `LogicError`
    // `rule:core-classes/db-streaming` promises, on the same connection, on the
    // same step.
    try {
        $db->query("select 1", []);
        echo "  a statement: not refused\n";
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        echo "  a statement: refused\n";
    }
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`: `db.connect` covers a SQLite block, whose path is configuration.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
row ada
  version: the same
  a statement: refused
row grace
  version: the same
  a statement: refused
